% Bencode: The BitTorrent Encoding Format
% Chapter 1 of the literate BitTorrent client

\section{Bencode}

Bencode (pronounced ``bee-encode'') is the encoding format used throughout the
BitTorrent protocol. Every \texttt{.torrent} file is bencoded, tracker responses
are bencoded, and even some peer protocol extensions use bencode. Understanding
bencode is therefore our first task.

\subsection{Why Not JSON or XML?}

Before diving into bencode's grammar, it's worth asking: why did Bram Cohen
invent a new serialization format instead of using an existing one like JSON
or XML? The answer lies in two properties that are essential for BitTorrent
but not guaranteed by general-purpose formats: \emph{canonical encoding} and
\emph{simplicity}.

\paragraph{Canonical Encoding.}
The most critical requirement is that bencode produces \emph{exactly one}
byte sequence for any given logical value. Consider JSON: the objects
\texttt{\{"a":1,"b":2\}} and \texttt{\{"b":2,"a":1\}} are semantically
identical, but they produce different byte sequences. Similarly, integers
can be written as \texttt{42}, \texttt{+42}, or \texttt{42.0}. Whitespace
is optional and can appear almost anywhere. These variations are harmless
for configuration files, but they are fatal for BitTorrent.

Why? Because the \emph{info hash}---the unique identifier for a torrent---is
computed by hashing the bencoded \texttt{info} dictionary. If two clients
encoded the same logical dictionary differently, they would compute different
hashes and be unable to find each other. The swarm would fragment based on
implementation details rather than content.

Bencode achieves canonical encoding through strict rules:
\begin{itemize}
\item Dictionary keys \emph{must} appear in sorted order (lexicographic by
  raw bytes).
\item Integers have exactly one representation (no leading zeros, no positive
  sign, no negative zero).
\item Byte strings are length-prefixed, leaving no room for escape sequences
  or encoding variations.
\item There is no optional whitespace anywhere in the format.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Simplicity.}
Bencode is also remarkably simple to implement. The entire grammar fits in
a few lines, and a complete encoder/decoder requires only a few hundred
lines of code in most languages. This matters for a protocol that must run
on embedded devices, be implemented by hobbyists, and remain stable for
decades. Complex formats like XML require sophisticated parsers and have
numerous edge cases; bencode has almost none.

The simplicity also aids debugging: bencoded data is mostly human-readable.
You can often understand a torrent file just by looking at its raw bytes,
without special tools.

\paragraph{Binary Safety.}
Unlike JSON (which assumes UTF-8 strings), bencode's byte strings are
arbitrary binary data. This is essential for storing SHA1 hashes (20 bytes
of binary data), peer IDs, and other non-textual content. There's no need
for Base64 encoding or other escaping mechanisms.

With these motivations in mind, let's examine the format itself.

\subsection{The Bencode Grammar}

Bencode is refreshingly simple. It has exactly four data types:

\begin{description}
\item[Integers] Encoded as \texttt{i<number>e}. For example, \texttt{i42e}
  represents 42, and \texttt{i-3e} represents $-3$. Leading zeros are forbidden
  (except for \texttt{i0e}), and \texttt{i-0e} is invalid.

\item[Byte strings] Encoded as \texttt{<length>:<data>}. The length is a
  non-negative integer in decimal ASCII, followed by a colon, followed by
  exactly that many bytes. For example, \texttt{4:spam} represents the bytes
  ``spam''. Note that byte strings are \emph{not} required to be valid UTF-8.

\item[Lists] Encoded as \texttt{l<items>e}. Items are concatenated with no
  separator. For example, \texttt{l4:spami42ee} is a list containing the
  string ``spam'' and the integer 42.

\item[Dictionaries] Encoded as \texttt{d<key><value>...e}. Keys must be byte
  strings and must appear in sorted order (lexicographic by raw bytes).
  For example, \texttt{d3:cow3:moo4:spam4:eggse} maps ``cow'' to ``moo''
  and ``spam'' to ``eggs''.
\end{description}

The sorted-key requirement for dictionaries is critical: it ensures that
any two encoders produce identical output for the same logical data. This
property is essential for computing the info hash, which is a SHA1 hash
of the bencoded info dictionary.

\subsubsection{Formal Grammar (EBNF)}

For those who prefer a formal specification, here is bencode expressed in
Extended Backus-Naur Form:

\begin{verbatim}
value       ::= integer | bytes | list | dict
integer     ::= 'i' int-value 'e'
int-value   ::= '0' | '-'? nonzero digit*
nonzero     ::= '1' | '2' | '3' | '4' | '5' | '6' | '7' | '8' | '9'
digit       ::= '0' | nonzero
bytes       ::= length ':' <length octets>
length      ::= '0' | nonzero digit*
list        ::= 'l' value* 'e'
dict        ::= 'd' (bytes value)* 'e'
\end{verbatim}

Note several constraints that the grammar encodes:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Integer values cannot have leading zeros (the [[int-value]] rule
  produces either ``0'' or a number starting with a non-zero digit).
\item Negative zero is forbidden: the optional minus sign only appears
  before [[nonzero]], never before ``0''.
\item String lengths cannot have leading zeros (same pattern as integers).
\item Dictionary keys must be byte strings (the grammar directly references
  [[bytes]], not [[value]]).
\end{enumerate}

The grammar does \emph{not} express the sorted-key constraint for dictionaries;
that is a semantic requirement that cannot be captured in context-free grammar.
Our decoder will accept unsorted keys (since [[BTreeMap]] sorts them anyway),
but our encoder will always produce correctly sorted output.

\subsection{The BencodeValue Type}

We represent bencoded data with a recursive enum. This type mirrors the
grammar directly---each variant corresponds to one of the four bencode types.

<<bencode types>>=
/// Represents a bencoded value.
///
/// Bencode supports four types: integers, byte strings, lists, and dictionaries.
/// This enum provides a type-safe representation that can be constructed from
/// parsing bencoded data or built programmatically for encoding.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum BencodeValue {
    /// A signed 64-bit integer (e.g., `i42e` or `i-3e`)
    Integer(i64),

    /// A byte string (e.g., `4:spam`)
    ///
    /// Note: Byte strings are NOT required to be valid UTF-8. In BitTorrent,
    /// they often contain binary data such as piece hashes or peer IDs.
    Bytes(Vec<u8>),

    /// An ordered list of values (e.g., `l4:spami42ee`)
    List(Vec<BencodeValue>),

    /// A dictionary mapping byte string keys to values (e.g., `d3:cow3:mooe`)
    ///
    /// We use `BTreeMap` rather than `HashMap` to maintain sorted key order.
    /// This is essential for computing canonical encodings, which in turn
    /// is required for reproducible info hash computation.
    Dict(std::collections::BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>),
}
@

We use [[BTreeMap]] for dictionaries rather than [[HashMap]] for an important
reason: the bencode specification requires dictionary keys to be sorted. By
using a sorted map, we ensure that iteration order matches the required
encoding order, making encoding straightforward and deterministic.

\subsection{Error Handling}

Parsing bencode can fail in several ways. We define a dedicated error type
to provide clear feedback about what went wrong and where. Using the
[[thiserror]] crate gives us ergonomic [[Display]] and [[Error]] implementations.

<<bencode errors>>=
/// Errors that can occur when parsing bencoded data.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum BencodeError {
    /// The input does not conform to bencode grammar
    #[error("invalid bencode format: {0}")]
    InvalidFormat(String),

    /// Unexpected end of input while parsing
    #[error("unexpected end of input")]
    UnexpectedEof,

    /// Integer parsing failed (invalid digits, leading zeros, etc.)
    #[error("invalid integer: {0}")]
    InvalidInteger(String),

    /// String length prefix is invalid (negative, overflow, etc.)
    #[error("invalid string length: {0}")]
    InvalidLength(String),

    /// Dictionary has a duplicate key
    #[error("duplicate key in dictionary: {0:?}")]
    DuplicateKey(Vec<u8>),

    /// Dictionary key is not a byte string
    #[error("dictionary key must be a byte string")]
    InvalidDictKey,

    /// Input contains extra bytes after the decoded value
    #[error("trailing data: {0} bytes remaining after value")]
    TrailingData(usize),
}
@

These error variants cover the main failure modes:

\begin{itemize}
\item [[InvalidFormat]]: Catch-all for structural problems like missing
  delimiters or invalid type markers.
\item [[UnexpectedEof]]: Input ended before a complete value was parsed.
\item [[InvalidInteger]]: The integer between \texttt{i} and \texttt{e} is
  malformed (e.g., leading zeros, non-digits).
\item [[InvalidLength]]: The length prefix of a byte string is invalid.
\item [[DuplicateKey]]: A dictionary contains the same key twice.
\item [[InvalidDictKey]]: A dictionary key is not a byte string.
\item [[TrailingData]]: Extra bytes remain after decoding a complete value.
\end{itemize}

