These tests serve as both verification and documentation. Reading through
them shows exactly what the decoder accepts and rejects, serving as a
specification complement to the prose description above.

\subsection{Property-Based Testing with Proptest}

While the unit tests above verify specific cases, property-based testing
explores the solution space more thoroughly. We use the [[proptest]] crate
to generate random [[BencodeValue]] instances and verify that our
encoder and decoder are true inverses.

The key properties we verify are:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Roundtrip (value to bytes)}: For any valid [[BencodeValue]] $v$,
  [[decode(encode(v)) == Ok(v)]]. This ensures the encoder produces valid
  bencode that the decoder can reconstruct perfectly.

\item \textbf{Roundtrip (bytes to value)}: For any valid encoded bytes $b$,
  [[encode(decode(b)) == Ok(b)]]. This ensures our encoding is canonical---there's
  exactly one way to encode any given value.
\end{enumerate}

The strategy for generating arbitrary [[BencodeValue]] instances uses
[[prop\_recursive]] to handle the recursive nature of lists and dictionaries.
We limit recursion depth and collection sizes to keep test execution fast
while still exploring interesting nested structures.

<<proptest imports>>=
use proptest::prelude::*;
use proptest::collection::{vec as prop_vec, btree_map as prop_btree_map};
@

<<proptest strategy>>=
/// Generates arbitrary BencodeValue instances for property testing.
///
/// The strategy uses prop_recursive to handle nested structures (lists and dicts)
/// while limiting depth and size to keep tests performant.
fn arb_bencode_value() -> impl Strategy<Value = BencodeValue> {
    // Leaf nodes: integers and byte strings
    let leaf = prop_oneof![
        any::<i64>().prop_map(BencodeValue::Integer),
        // Limit byte string size to avoid excessive memory usage
        prop_vec(any::<u8>(), 0..64).prop_map(BencodeValue::Bytes),
    ];

    // Recursively build nested structures
    // Parameters: depth=4, desired_size=64, expected_branch_size=8
    leaf.prop_recursive(4, 64, 8, |inner| {
        prop_oneof![
            // Lists containing 0-8 elements
            prop_vec(inner.clone(), 0..8).prop_map(BencodeValue::List),
            // Dicts with 0-8 entries; keys are byte strings
            prop_btree_map(prop_vec(any::<u8>(), 0..32), inner, 0..8)
                .prop_map(BencodeValue::Dict),
        ]
    })
}
