The [[proptest\_config]] annotation ensures we run at least 1000 test cases,
meeting our coverage requirements. Proptest's shrinking capability will
automatically find minimal failing cases when a bug is discovered, making
debugging significantly easier.

Note that we test canonical encoding by generating values, encoding them,
decoding, and re-encoding. This avoids the complexity of generating arbitrary
valid bencode bytes directly (which would require implementing the grammar
as a generator).

\subsection{Encoding Bencode Values}

Encoding is the inverse of decoding: we traverse a [[BencodeValue]] tree and
produce a byte sequence following the bencode grammar. Notice the symmetric
structure between encoder and decoder: where the decoder inspects the first
byte to dispatch to type-specific parsing functions, the encoder matches on
the [[BencodeValue]] variant to dispatch to type-specific output logic.

This symmetry is not accidental---it reflects the clean correspondence between
the grammar and our data representation. Each grammar production becomes a
decoder function (consuming bytes to produce a value) and an encoder arm
(consuming a value to produce bytes):

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcl}
\textbf{Grammar} & & \textbf{Code} \\
\hline
\texttt{integer} & $\leftrightarrow$ & [[decode\_integer]] / [[BencodeValue::Integer]] \\
\texttt{bytes} & $\leftrightarrow$ & [[decode\_bytes]] / [[BencodeValue::Bytes]] \\
\texttt{list} & $\leftrightarrow$ & [[decode\_list]] / [[BencodeValue::List]] \\
\texttt{dict} & $\leftrightarrow$ & [[decode\_dict]] / [[BencodeValue::Dict]] \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Encoding is simpler than decoding because we don't need to handle errors---a
well-formed [[BencodeValue]] always produces valid bencode output. There are
no malformed inputs to reject, no unexpected bytes to diagnose. We simply
walk the tree and emit bytes.

The encoding rules mirror the grammar:

\begin{description}
\item[Integers] Write \texttt{i}, the decimal digits, then \texttt{e}.
\item[Byte strings] Write the length as decimal digits, a colon, then the raw bytes.
\item[Lists] Write \texttt{l}, encode each element, then write \texttt{e}.
\item[Dictionaries] Write \texttt{d}, encode each key-value pair (keys are already
  sorted by [[BTreeMap]]), then write \texttt{e}.
\end{description}

We provide two functions: [[encode\_to]] writes to an existing buffer for
efficiency (avoiding allocations when encoding multiple values or when the
caller wants to control the buffer), and [[encode]] is a convenience wrapper
that creates a new buffer and returns it.

<<encode>>=
/// Encodes a `BencodeValue` into the provided buffer.
///
/// This is the efficient encoding function that appends to an existing buffer,
/// avoiding allocation when encoding multiple values or when the caller wants
/// control over the output buffer.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `value` - The bencode value to encode
/// * `out` - The buffer to append the encoded bytes to
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::bencode::{encode_to, BencodeValue};
///
/// let mut buf = Vec::new();
/// encode_to(&BencodeValue::Integer(42), &mut buf);
/// assert_eq!(buf, b"i42e");
///
/// // Append another value
/// encode_to(&BencodeValue::Bytes(b"spam".to_vec()), &mut buf);
/// assert_eq!(buf, b"i42e4:spam");
/// ```
pub fn encode_to(value: &BencodeValue, out: &mut Vec<u8>) {
    match value {
        BencodeValue::Integer(n) => {
            // Format: i<number>e
            out.push(b'i');
            out.extend_from_slice(n.to_string().as_bytes());
            out.push(b'e');
        }
        BencodeValue::Bytes(bytes) => {
            // Format: <length>:<data>
            out.extend_from_slice(bytes.len().to_string().as_bytes());
            out.push(b':');
            out.extend_from_slice(bytes);
        }
        BencodeValue::List(items) => {
            // Format: l<items>e
            out.push(b'l');
            for item in items {
                encode_to(item, out);
            }
            out.push(b'e');
        }
        BencodeValue::Dict(entries) => {
            // Format: d<key><value>...e
            // BTreeMap iterates in sorted key order, which is exactly what
            // bencode requires for canonical encoding.
            out.push(b'd');
            for (key, val) in entries {
                // Key is a byte string
                out.extend_from_slice(key.len().to_string().as_bytes());
                out.push(b':');
                out.extend_from_slice(key);
                // Value can be any type
                encode_to(val, out);
            }
            out.push(b'e');
        }
    }
}

/// Encodes a `BencodeValue` into a new byte vector.
///
/// This is a convenience wrapper around [`encode_to`] that allocates a new
/// buffer and returns it. Use [`encode_to`] directly if you want to control
/// the buffer or encode multiple values into the same buffer.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `value` - The bencode value to encode
///
/// # Returns
///
/// A `Vec<u8>` containing the bencoded representation.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::bencode::{encode, decode, BencodeValue};
///
/// // Encode an integer
/// let encoded = encode(&BencodeValue::Integer(42));
/// assert_eq!(encoded, b"i42e");
///
/// // Round-trip: encode then decode
/// let original = BencodeValue::Bytes(b"hello".to_vec());
/// let encoded = encode(&original);
/// let decoded = decode(&encoded).unwrap();
/// assert_eq!(decoded, original);
/// ```
pub fn encode(value: &BencodeValue) -> Vec<u8> {
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    encode_to(value, &mut out);
    out
}
@

The implementation is straightforward because [[BencodeValue]] already
represents well-formed data. There's no need for error handling---every
possible [[BencodeValue]] produces valid bencode output. The recursive
structure mirrors the decoder: [[encode\_to]] calls itself for nested
lists and dictionaries.

One important property: because [[BTreeMap]] maintains keys in sorted order,
dictionary encoding automatically produces canonical output. This is critical
for computing the info hash, where the exact byte sequence matters.

\subsection{The Round-Trip Property and Info Hash Computation}

A well-implemented bencode library must satisfy the \emph{round-trip property}:
for any valid bencode byte sequence $b$ and any [[BencodeValue]] $v$,

\begin{align}
\text{decode}(\text{encode}(v)) &= \text{Ok}(v) \label{eq:roundtrip1} \\
\text{encode}(\text{decode}(b)) &= b \label{eq:roundtrip2}
\end{align}

Property~\eqref{eq:roundtrip1} states that encoding preserves semantics: we can
always recover the original value from its encoding. Property~\eqref{eq:roundtrip2}
is stronger---it states that our encoding is \emph{canonical}: there is exactly
one byte sequence for any given value, and decoding then re-encoding produces
that exact sequence.

\paragraph{Why This Matters for BitTorrent.}
The canonical encoding property is not merely aesthetic; it is essential for
the protocol's operation. The \emph{info hash} is computed as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Parse the \texttt{.torrent} file to extract the \texttt{info} dictionary.
\item Re-encode that dictionary using the bencode encoder.
\item Compute SHA1 of the resulting bytes.
\end{enumerate}

If our encoder produced different bytes than the original \texttt{.torrent}
file's info section, we would compute the wrong hash. Our client would be
unable to connect to the swarm because it would be looking for peers sharing
a different (nonexistent) torrent.

This is why property~\eqref{eq:roundtrip2} is so critical. When we decode
the info dictionary from a torrent file and re-encode it, we \emph{must}
get the exact same bytes. Any deviation---different key ordering, different
integer formatting, anything---would produce a different hash.

\paragraph{Verification Through Testing.}
Our property-based tests (using [[proptest]]) verify both round-trip properties
across thousands of randomly generated values. If either property fails, we
have a bug that could cause real protocol failures. The test
[[proptest\_roundtrip\_decode\_encode]] specifically checks canonical encoding:
we encode a value, decode it, re-encode, and verify the bytes are identical.

\paragraph{Implications for Implementation.}
The round-trip requirement influenced several design decisions:

\begin{itemize}
\item We use [[BTreeMap]] rather than [[HashMap]] for dictionaries, ensuring
  keys iterate in sorted order without explicit sorting during encoding.
\item We reject leading zeros in integers and string lengths during decoding,
  matching the strictness of canonical encoding.
\item We use Rust's [[i64::to\_string()]] for integer encoding, which produces
  the minimal representation (no leading zeros, no positive sign).
\end{itemize}

These choices ensure that our encoder produces the unique canonical form for
any value, making round-trip through our implementation lossless.
